# Build context for backend/Dockerfile.
#
# That Dockerfile's context is the REPOSITORY ROOT rather than backend/, because Alembic needs
# alembic.ini and database/migrations/ and both live here. A root context would otherwise
# sweep in everything else as well -- .git, .venv, node_modules, the frontend, the test suite
# -- so this file is what keeps the context to what the image actually copies.
#
# ## Three rules that decide whether a pattern here does what it looks like it does
#
#   1. Patterns are relative to the CONTEXT ROOT. `tests` means ./tests and nothing else.
#
#   2. Matching is Go's filepath.Match, where `*` DOES NOT CROSS `/`. So a bare `__pycache__`
#      excludes only a __pycache__ at the root, and `*.pyc` only a .pyc at the root. To reach
#      nested ones the pattern needs `**/`, which is Docker's own extension and matches any
#      number of directories including zero. This is not pedantry: without `**/` the context
#      here carries 132 stale .pyc files from 15 nested __pycache__ directories inside
#      backend/app and database/migrations, compiled by the developer's Python 3.14 and copied
#      into a Python 3.12 image that cannot use them.
#
#   3. A later line overrides an earlier one, which is why every `!` negation sits after the
#      pattern it re-admits.
#
# This file governs the backend build only. The frontend service builds from ./frontend with
# its own frontend/.dockerignore; excluding `frontend` here does not affect that build.
#
# (backend/.dockerignore still exists but is now DEAD: Docker reads only the .dockerignore at
# the context root. Everything it used to exclude is covered below.)

# --- MUST NOT be excluded ---------------------------------------------------
#
#   backend/requirements.txt    COPY backend/requirements.txt ./
#   backend/app/                COPY backend/app ./app
#   alembic.ini                 COPY alembic.ini ./
#   database/migrations/        COPY database/migrations ./database/migrations
#
# No pattern below may match those four. One that did would remove them from the context and
# the build would fail at COPY -- loudly, which is the single mercy of that mistake.

# --- version control --------------------------------------------------------
.git
.gitattributes
.gitignore
.github

# --- Python environments ----------------------------------------------------
.venv
venv
env
ENV

# --- bytecode and tool caches, at every depth -------------------------------
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.tox
**/.nox
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov

# --- not inputs to the API image --------------------------------------------
#
# The frontend builds from its own context. tests/, docs/ and scripts/ are not imported by
# backend/app -- verified rather than assumed: app/ imports only the standard library, the
# declared third-party packages, and (since Stage 6.6) the offline `ml` package through exactly
# one module, backend/app/ml/artifact_store.py.
frontend
tests
docs
scripts

# --- the offline ML package, narrowed rather than excluded (Stage 6.7) ------
#
# `ml` was excluded outright until Stage 6.7. It no longer can be: backend/Dockerfile's
# disposable `artifact-builder` stage regenerates the approved model from the committed dataset,
# which means the dataset and the offline package both have to be reachable IN THE CONTEXT.
#
# The context is not the image. The builder stage is thrown away, and the `runtime` stage copies
# an explicit allowlist of thirteen modules plus the two artifact files -- so nothing below is
# what keeps the dataset out of what ships. That is the Dockerfile's job, and a CI step audits
# the built image for it.
#
# What IS excluded here is everything no stage has any use for:
#
#   ml/data/raw, ml/data/external   17 MB of source data the processed CSV was derived from.
#                                   Nothing in the build reads them.
#   ml/notebooks                    exploratory work; never an input to anything.
#   **/*.pkl                        the safety line. The payload is generated, never committed,
#                                   and a developer's working tree usually HAS one at
#                                   ml/models/demand_baseline_v1/model.pkl. Without this, a
#                                   local `docker build` would sweep that file into the context
#                                   and the image could end up carrying a payload nobody
#                                   verified. Excluded at every depth, so no future COPY can
#                                   reach one either.
ml/data/raw
ml/data/external
ml/notebooks
**/*.pkl

# database/init/ is bind-mounted into the db service at runtime and is not part of this image.
# database/migrations/ IS part of it, and is deliberately not matched here.
database/init

# --- deployment descriptors -------------------------------------------------
# In the context but never copied; excluding them stops an unrelated compose edit from
# invalidating a build-cache layer.
#
# backend/Dockerfile and this file are deliberately NOT listed. Both can be excluded -- Docker
# special-cases them so the build still works -- but that special case is exactly the kind of
# thing this stage cannot verify with Docker unavailable, and the saving is a few kilobytes.
docker-compose.yml
backend/.dockerignore
README.md

# --- secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every dotenv form, at every depth. Compose and Vite both read mode files such as
# `.env.production`, and a working tree may hold one. None is copied by the Dockerfile, but a
# file that never enters the build context cannot be copied by a future edit either, and
# cannot surface in an exported context.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
# Root only. A `!**/.env.example` here would be a mistake: the last matching pattern wins, so
# it would re-admit frontend/.env.example and quietly undo the `frontend` exclusion above.
!.env.example

**/*.pem
**/*.key

# The certificate directory, by name as well as by extension.
#
# `TLS_CERT_DIR` defaults to `./certs` (docs/deployment/tls.md), so an operator following the
# runbook creates that directory inside this very build context. The two extensions above catch
# the fullchain.pem and privkey.pem it usually holds -- but a CA hands out .crt, .cer, .pfx and
# .p12 as well, and none of those was matched by anything in this file.
certs
**/*.crt
**/*.cer
**/*.pfx
**/*.p12
**/*.jks

# Database dumps.
#
# docs/deployment/backup-restore.md tells the operator to write
# `./hotel-intelligence-<stamp>.backup` INTO THE REPOSITORY ROOT, which is exactly this context.
# Nothing here copies it, but a dump of a real database is the single worst file to leave where
# a future COPY -- or an exported context -- could pick it up.
**/*.backup
**/*.dump

# --- editors / OS -----------------------------------------------------------
.vscode
.idea
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
**/*.swp

# --- local data -------------------------------------------------------------
**/*.db
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3
